  <h2>{nav.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {nav.revision} · How a military pilot gets the aircraft where it needs to go: reading the HSI, setting up and flying a course, intercepts, DME arcs, point to point and waypoint steering, with positions, headings, wind and the navigation aids as background. Written for military pilots of every service from US Navy training publications, with the Army field manual for the military grid and the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) rules that every aircraft follows in US airspace as the supplementary source. The T-45C examples carry across to any military aircraft; the aircraft's NATOPS or service flight manual governs its own systems.</p>
  <p>Start with Figure 1: an animated plan view beside the HSI, with scenarios for crossing a course, the Navy intercept methods, wind, <Term id="Homing">homing</Term> and <Term id="Station passage">station passage</Term>, and a mode where you fly it yourself.</p>
 </section>
