 </nav>

 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>This module is about getting the aircraft where it needs to go: setting a course on the horizontal situation indicator (HSI), flying to and from a station, intercepting a course, flying DME arcs and point to point, and following waypoints. The Navy's instrument syllabus teaches it after aircraft control, as the skill of identifying your position relative to navigation aids and then flying airways on real cross-country flights. Its rule for all of it is "Aviate, Navigate, Communicate": fly the aircraft first, and do not let navigation take attention from basic airwork. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('1')}</a> How positions, headings and the navigation aids themselves work is collected at the end, under <a href="#background">Background</a>.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: read where you are and where the course is from the HSI picture; tune, identify and set up a course; fly to and from a <Term>TACAN</Term> or VOR station and correct for wind; intercept a course inbound or outbound with the right lead; join, fly and leave a DME arc; fly point to point; fly waypoints and go direct to one; and cross-check one source against another.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a substitute for your aircraft's NATOPS or service flight manual, your unit's procedures, or current flight information publications. Worked examples use the T-45C training jet's displays from the Navy's instrument flight training instruction; other aircraft present the same information differently. Holding and instrument approaches belong to instrument flight; the carrier version of point-to-point <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> navigation is in the <a href={`${caseIii}#tacan`}>Case III module</a>. Weapons-quality target coordinates, with elevation and datum checks for weapons delivery, are outside this module.</p>

 <h2 id="hsi">Reading the HSI</h2>
 <p>The T-45C's HSI is a heading-up compass rose with the aircraft symbol fixed in the centre. On it: <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12–13')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li>The <strong>bearing pointer</strong>: its head points to the station and its tail sits on the <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> you are on. The <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> pointer is on the outer edge of the rose; the VOR or waypoint pointer on the inner edge.</li>
  <li>The <strong>course line</strong> and course deviation indicator (CDI): with <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> or VOR steering, each dot is 5° off the selected course. The bar is on the side the course lies, so fly toward the bar.</li>
  <li>A <strong>planimetric course line</strong> option draws the course itself through the station symbol, showing intercept angle and distance from the course directly; the rose's range scale is selectable.</li>
  <li>Digital bearing, <Term id="Slant range">slant range</Term> and time to go for the selected <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> station or waypoint.</li>
 </ul>
 <p><strong>Reading the dots.</strong> The dots either side of the centre are a scale for how far the bar is off. On the T-45C, with <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> or VOR steering, each dot is 5° from the selected course; the bar starts to move within 10° of it, so on a two-dot scale like Figure 1's the bar is at full scale, against the end of the scale, when you are 10° or more off, and a pinned bar does not tell you how much more. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('13, 66')}</a> Degrees are not distance: at 60 NM from the station a degree is about 1 NM, so one dot is about 5 NM off course, while at 30 NM one dot is about 2½ NM. With waypoint steering the same dots measure distance instead: full scale is 4.0 NM either side of the track with the gear up and 0.3 NM with the gear down. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('13')}</a> Other displays use other scales. A typical civil VOR indicator has five dots each side, 2° a dot, and is at full scale at 12° or more off. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('9-13')}</a> Check what a dot means on your own aircraft.</p>
 <p>Because the <Term id="Radial">radials</Term> spread apart with distance, 1 NM apart at 60 NM and ½ NM apart at 30 NM, the CDI moves fast close to a station and slowly far from it. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('66')}</a> DME is <Term id="Slant range">slant range</Term>, not horizontal distance. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-7')}</a> <strong><Term id="Station passage">Station passage</Term>:</strong> with VOR, when the bearing pointer falls past the 90° benchmark on the rose. <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> has a larger cone of confusion over the station, so <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> <Term id="Station passage">station passage</Term> is when DME stops decreasing, at minimum DME. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('65')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Read it as a map.</strong> The HSI is a picture: the fixed aircraft symbol and the course deviation bar show the aircraft relative to the selected course as though you were above the aircraft looking down. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('5-23')}</a> The bar is the course and the symbol in the middle is you, so an aircraft symbol pointing across the bar is an aircraft flying across the course. The T-45C's planimetric course line option draws the same picture with distance to scale. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('13')}</a> Figure 1 puts a plan view beside the HSI so you can watch the two move together.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="fly" aria-labelledby="fly-title">
