 <h2 id="setup">Setting up a course</h2>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Tune and identify.</strong> Select the <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> channel or VOR frequency and listen to the identifier before you use it. The identifier is the only positive identification of a VOR; during maintenance it may send T-E-S-T or nothing. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-3')}</a> Disregard an aid that a <Term>NOTAM</Term> says is unusable, however good it looks. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-1')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Find yourself.</strong> The tail of the bearing pointer is the <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> you are on; its head is the bearing to the station. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12–13')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Set the course the way you will fly it.</strong> Set the inbound course to fly to the station and the <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> to fly away from it. Set to the reciprocal, a CDI reverses its sense; set the course to agree with the way you intend to fly and it never does. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('9-14')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Check TO or FROM.</strong> TO, pointing at the head of the course arrow, means the course takes you to the station if you intercept and fly it; FROM means it takes you away. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('5-23')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Read the picture.</strong> The bar is on the side the course lies, so turn toward it. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12–13')}</a> Then pick an intercept, below.</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="tracking">Flying to and from a station</h2>
 <p><strong>Direct to the station.</strong> Tune and identify it, turn the shorter way to put the bearing pointer at the top, centre the course, and then put the ground-track marker under the lubber line and crab as needed to keep the course centred. Keeping the pointer at the top without correcting for wind is <Term id="Homing">homing</Term>, and in a crosswind it flies a curved path. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('64')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Correcting for wind.</strong> The angle between the lubber line and the ground-track marker is your crab angle. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('65')}</a> Without a ground-track marker, bracket: on the course, a bar drifting left means a crosswind from the left. Turn toward it, starting with 20°; when the bar centres again, take out half of the correction, and keep halving as the drift slows. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('9-14')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term id="Station passage">Station passage</Term>.</strong> Close to the station the CDI moves fast, because the <Term id="Radial">radials</Term> converge, <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('66')}</a> and rapid or fluctuating movement means <Term id="Station passage">station passage</Term> is near. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('9-14')}</a> With VOR, passage is when the bearing pointer falls past the 90° benchmark; <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term>'s cone of confusion is larger, so <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> passage is minimum DME. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('65')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Outbound.</strong> Set the <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> you want and either fly over the station or hold an intercept heading until the CDI centres, then track it outbound as you did inbound. <a href="#source-ifh">{ifh('9-14')}</a> Figure 1 has scenarios for <Term id="Homing">homing</Term> and for tracking in the same crosswind, and for <Term id="Station passage">station passage</Term>.</p>

 <h2 id="intercepts">Intercepting a course</h2>
 <p>The intercept angle is the angle between your heading and the course. It is normally greater than the number of degrees you are off course, and it must not exceed 90°, where the rate of closure is greatest. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('65')}</a> The FTI's methods: <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('66–70')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>{INTERCEPT_METHODS.thirty.label}.</strong> Set the course, look from it to the head of the bearing pointer and 30° beyond: that is the intercept heading.</li>
  <li><strong>{INTERCEPT_METHODS.double.label}.</strong> Look from the course to the head of the pointer and as many degrees again beyond it, at most 45°, so the intercept can be anything from a degree or two to 90°. Weigh your distance from the NAVAID, <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> spread and wind when you choose between this and the 30° method.</li>
  <li><strong>{INTERCEPT_METHODS.passage.label}.</strong> After passage turn parallel to the course, set it, look from the tail of the pointer to the course and as many degrees again beyond, at most 45°.</li>
  <li><strong>{INTERCEPT_METHODS.fortyfive.label}.</strong> For an outbound <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> some distance away: look from the tail of the pointer to the course and 45° beyond.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Turn onto the course before you reach it. The <Term id="Lead point">lead point</Term> is 1% of ground speed for a 90° intercept and a proportion of that for smaller angles: a 45° intercept needs a third as much lead. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('65–66')}</a> Watch the bearing pointer and the course line closing to judge the turn.</p>
 <p>The FTI-method scenarios in Figure 1 fly the 30° and double-the-angle methods inbound and the 45° method outbound.</p>
 <h2 id="dme">Using distance: ground speed and arcs</h2>
 <p><strong>Ground speed check.</strong> Fly directly to or from the station, with the DME at least your altitude in thousands of feet so <Term id="Slant range">slant range</Term> does not distort it. Start timing on a whole DME number, note the change after a set time, and divide: 12 NM in 2 minutes is 6 NM a minute, 360 kt; 12 NM in 3 minutes is 240 kt. Timing longer improves accuracy. The T-45C's GPS/inertial system computes ground speed anywhere; its <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> time to go uses <Term id="Slant range">slant range</Term>, so it is valid only under the same DME rule, while waypoint time to go is not <Term id="Slant range">slant range</Term>. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('71')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Joining an arc.</strong> The turn from a <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> onto an arc is normally about 90°. Lead it by 1% of ground speed: at 250 kt start the turn 2.5 NM early, adding the lead to the arc DME when inbound and subtracting it outbound. Use a half-standard-rate turn and roll out when the bearing pointer nears the 90° benchmark. For turns of about 45° use a third of that lead, for about 60° two-thirds, and for 30° or less very little. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('72')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Flying the arc.</strong> The <em>chord method</em>, usually beyond 12 DME, flies short straight legs: roll out with the bearing pointer 5–10° ahead of the 90° benchmark, hold the heading until it falls 5–10° behind, and turn again. The <em>angle-of-bank method</em>, usually inside 12 DME, holds the pointer on the benchmark with a shallow turn; the closer the station, the steeper the bank. Correct 5–10° for each ½ NM off the arc, and adjust the pointer position for wind. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('73–74')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Leaving the arc for a <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>.</strong> Lead in <Term id="Radial">radials</Term>: divide 60 by the arc DME and multiply by 1% of ground speed. On a 15 DME arc at 250 kt that is 60 ÷ 15 × 2.5 = 10 <Term id="Radial">radials</Term>. Turn at a half standard rate, varying the bank (never more than 30°) to centre the course as you roll out. Inside 10 DME the turn is more than 90° inbound and less outbound, and the FTI corrects the lead: <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('74–76')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Approximate lead radials at 250 knots ground speed" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Approximate lead radials at 250 KGS, from the FTI <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('76')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Arc DME</th><th scope="col">90° turn</th><th scope="col">Inbound turn (&gt;90°)</th><th scope="col">Outbound turn (&lt;90°)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{ARC_LEAD_TABLE.map(r => <tr><th scope="row">{r.dme}</th><td>{r.ninety}</td><td>{r.inbound ?? '—'}</td><td>{r.outbound ?? '—'}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="arc" aria-labelledby="arc-title">
  <h3 id="arc-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — Join, fly and leave a DME arc</h3>
  <p>Outbound on the 180 <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>, join the arc clockwise and leave it for the 270 <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>. The figure marks the turn onto the arc, flies the arc by chords, and marks the lead <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>.</p>
