  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">No wind. Chord turns are drawn as instant. The DME band the chords fly is computed geometry, not an FTI figure.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="p2p">Point to point</h2>
 <p>Cleared direct to a fix, you can use radar vectors, traditional point-to-point navigation or waypoint offset navigation. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('76')}</a> The traditional method uses the HSI as a plotting board: <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('77–78')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li>Tune and identify the <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> or VOR/DME station. Picture the station at the centre of the rose.</li>
  <li>Put the fix that is farther from the station at the edge of the rose on its <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>, and the nearer one at a proportional distance on its own <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>. Your own position is on the tail of the bearing pointer.</li>
  <li>Join the two points with a line, move that line parallel to itself through the centre of the rose, and read the course where it crosses the edge.</li>
  <li>Turn to that course with any known wind correction, and repeat the solution periodically en route.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>With a waypoint system, waypoint offset navigation does the work: select the waypoint that is the NAVAID defining the fix and enter the fix's bearing and range from it. Steering then goes to the offset point. On the T-45C range runs 0.0–99.9 NM and bearing 000–359.9°; changing the waypoint's position clears its offset. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('79')}</a> The Case III module uses the same geometry to reach the <a href={`${caseIii}#tacan`}>marshal fix</a>.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="p2p" aria-labelledby="p2p-title">
  <h3 id="p2p-title" class="fig-title">Figure 3 — Point to point on the HSI</h3>
  <p>Set your fix and the target fix. Estimate the course with the plotting-board method, then check it.</p>
