  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Flat-earth geometry with horizontal range and no wind, as in the Case III module.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="waypoints">Waypoints, offsets and direct-to</h2>
 <p>Area navigation (RNAV) lets an aircraft fly any desired path within the coverage of ground- or space-based aids or the limits of self-contained ones. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-1')}</a> Its building blocks: <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-1')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li>A <strong>waypoint</strong> is a predetermined position defined by latitude and longitude; it can be a named point or a NAVAID, intersection or fix, and usually marks a change of direction, speed or altitude.</li>
  <li><strong>Fly-by</strong> waypoints let the aircraft begin the turn to the next course before reaching the point (turn anticipation). <strong>Fly-over</strong> waypoints must be crossed before the turn begins.</li>
  <li><strong>Leg types</strong> describe the path: track to fix (TF), also called point-to-point, between two waypoints; <Term id="Direct to fix">direct to fix</Term> (DF) from wherever the aircraft is ("turn right direct BRAVO"); course to fix (CF); radius to fix (RF); and heading legs.</li>
 </ul>
 <p><strong>On the T-45C,</strong> the selected steering, whether <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term>, VOR, waypoint or waypoint offset, drives the HSI's bearing pointer and data block. With waypoint steering the CDI shows cross-track distance: full scale is ±4.0 NM with the gear up and ±0.3 NM with the gear down. A sequential steering string of two or more waypoints can be drawn as a dashed line. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12–13')}</a> Waypoints come from the mission data loader, whose 200 stored points can be transferred into the tactical waypoints and must then be compared to check the transfer, or are entered by hand as latitude, longitude, elevation and <Term id="Variation">magnetic variation</Term>. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('79–80')}</a> Other aircraft name and lay out the same functions differently; your NATOPS manual governs.</p>
 <p>Check the database: it must be current for instrument use, and waypoints must match the chart's names, order and logical positions. Manual entry of waypoints by latitude and longitude is not permitted for approach procedures. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-17')}</a></p>
