 <h2 id="crosscheck">Cross-checking and degraded navigation</h2>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Identify every aid.</strong> The only positive identification of a VOR is its Morse code or recorded voice identifier; during maintenance it may send T-E-S-T or no identifier. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-3')}</a> Disregard indications from an aid that is NOTAMed out of service. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-1')}</a> An ADF has no failure flag, so monitor the NDB's identifier. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Cross-check GPS.</strong> Routine position checks against VOR or DME can detect a compromised GPS signal. Before flight, check <Term id="NOTAM">NOTAMs</Term>, plan fuel contingencies and plan to use conventional aids at the destination; in flight, watch for changes in navigation performance, clock errors and wrong positions, and tell ATC about apparent GPS degradation. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-4')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Keep conventional skills.</strong> The FAA keeps a minimum network of VORs so that aircraft can navigate through a GPS outage or land without GPS; pilots of GPS-equipped aircraft should stay proficient in VOR and ILS approaches. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-3')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Communication when navigation fails, and lost-communication routes, are in the <a href={`${radio}#nordo`}>Radio Procedures module</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="background">Background</h2>
 <p>The rest of this module is the background to everything above: how a position is written, how headings relate to true and magnetic north, how wind is worked out on the ground, what each navigation aid gives you, and how satellite and inertial systems work.</p>

 <h2 id="earth">Position on the earth</h2>
 <p>The earth is not a perfect sphere: its diameter at the equator is about 6,888 nautical miles (NM) and through the poles about 6,865 NM. For navigation it is treated as a sphere, because the difference is small enough to be insignificant. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-3')}</a> A position on it is given by a grid of geographic coordinates: <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-4')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Latitude</strong> locates a point north or south of the equator, from 0° at the equator to 90° at the poles. Parallels of latitude are always parallel to the plane of the equator, and "north" or "south" must always be stated.</li>
  <li><strong>Longitude</strong> locates a point east or west of the prime meridian through Greenwich, England, from 0° to 180° east or west. Meridians are halves of great circles through the poles, and "east" or "west" must always be stated.</li>
  <li>A <strong>great circle</strong> is the intersection of the earth's surface with a plane through its centre. The equator is one; every meridian is half of one.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Distance in navigation is the international nautical mile, 6,076 ft, longer than the 5,280 ft statute mile. One minute of arc along a meridian, which is one minute of latitude, is 1 NM. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-7')}</a> Because meridians converge toward the poles, a minute of longitude is 1 NM only at the equator and shrinks toward the poles; for the same reason a course is measured against a meridian near the middle of the route, not at the departure point. <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-5')}</a> Time is kept as Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), "Zulu", the time at the Greenwich meridian, on the 24-hour clock. <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-5')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="coords">Writing a position</h2>
 <p>The same position can be written several ways, and navigation systems accept several of them. The Army's map-reading manual lists the formats a GPS receiver can show: degrees, minutes and seconds (DMS); degrees and decimal minutes (DM); Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) grid eastings and northings in metres; and MGRS references. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('J-2')}</a> T-45C waypoints are entered as latitude and longitude, with the elevation and <Term id="Variation">magnetic variation</Term> of the point. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('80')}</a> Check which format and how many decimals a system expects before you type: N30°21.2' (degrees and minutes) and N30.212° (decimal degrees) are about 8.5 NM apart.</p>
 <p><strong>The military grid.</strong> MGRS shortens UTM coordinates by replacing several numbers with letters, and it makes a reference unique worldwide. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('4-12')}</a> A reference is built from large to small: <a href="#source-fm">{fm('4-12–4-13, 4-21–4-23')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong><Term id="Grid zone designation">Grid zone designation</Term>.</strong> Between 80°S and 84°N the earth is divided into 60 zones, each 6° of longitude wide, numbered 1 to 60 west to east from the 180° meridian, and into 20 rows lettered C to X (I and O are left out) from south to north. The rows are 8° high except X, which is 12°. Number then letter: "16S".</li>
  <li><strong>100,000-metre square.</strong> Two letters, column then row, unique within the grid zone: "16S GL".</li>
  <li><strong>Easting and northing.</strong> Digits within the square, always an even number of them, read right then up: the first half is the easting, the second the northing. Each added pair divides the square by ten: 10,000, 1,000, 100, 10 metres. The digits name the south-west corner of the square that contains the point.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>Coordinates are written as one continuous number, without spaces or punctuation, and the 100,000-metre square letters must always be included. Locations are normally reported to 100 m (six digits) and targets to 10 m (eight digits); GPS receivers give 4-, 6-, 8- and 10-digit references. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('4-21, J-2')}</a> The polar areas use the Universal Polar Stereographic (UPS) grid and its letters A, B, Y and Z instead. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('4-13')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Datum.</strong> Every coordinate is relative to a horizontal datum. Check the datum note on every chart, and that a receiver uses the same datum as the chart: UTM coordinates of the same point computed on different datums can differ by as much as 900 metres. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('3-3')}</a></p>
