 <h2 id="direction">Direction: true, magnetic and compass</h2>
 <p>Direction is measured clockwise in degrees from a north reference, from 000 through east 090, south 180 and west 270. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-5')}</a> Which north matters: a heading measured from true north is a true heading (TH), from magnetic north a magnetic heading (MH), and from the compass's own north a compass heading (CH), so always say which reference you mean. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-6')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term id="Variation">Variation</Term></strong> is the angle between true north and magnetic north. It is east when magnetic north lies east of true north and west when it lies west, and it differs from place to place. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-6')}</a> Charts show it as isogonic lines; on the agonic line it is zero. To go from true to magnetic, subtract east <Term id="Variation">variation</Term> and add west: "east is least, west is best". <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-6–16-8')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Deviation</strong> is the compass's own error from the aircraft's magnetic fields: metal, electrical current and equipment turn with the aircraft, so deviation changes with heading. It is measured by swinging the compass on a compass rose, and a deviation card near the compass gives the correction. <Term id="Variation">Variation</Term> plus deviation is compass error. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-6–2-7')}</a> <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-8')}</a></li>
  <li>The sequence is TH ± <Term id="Variation">variation</Term> = MH ± deviation = CH. <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-9')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p><strong>Heading, course, track and bearing</strong> are different things. Heading is where the nose points. Course is the intended path over the ground; track is the actual path; the angle between heading and track is drift. A bearing is the direction from the aircraft to an object. <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-9–16-10')}</a> A <Term id="Relative bearing">relative bearing</Term> is measured clockwise from the aircraft's nose to the object, so an object directly behind is at 180 on any heading; a true bearing is measured from true north. Heading plus <Term id="Relative bearing">relative bearing</Term> gives the true bearing. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-11–2-13')}</a></p>
