 <h2 id="wind">Dead reckoning and the wind triangle</h2>
 <p>Dead reckoning is navigation by computation from time, airspeed, distance and direction; corrected for wind, it gives the heading to fly and the ground speed (GS). <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-13')}</a> An aircraft moves through the air, and the air moves over the ground: at 120 kt true airspeed (TAS) with a 20 kt tailwind the ground speed is 140 kt; with a 20 kt headwind it is 100 kt; with a crosswind the aircraft drifts. To stay on course, head into the wind by the wind correction angle (WCA). <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-9')}</a></p>
 <p>The wind triangle solves it: one hour of airspeed along the heading plus one hour of wind gives the track and ground speed. The PHAK's example: true course 090°, wind from 045° at 40 kt, TAS 120 kt gives a WCA of 14° left, a true heading of 076° and a ground speed of 88 kt, so 220 NM takes 2 hours 30 minutes. <a href="#source-phak">{ph('16-14–16-15')}</a> In flight the T-45C shows the same thing directly: its HSI shows ground speed, wind and a ground-track marker, and the angle between the lubber line and the ground-track marker is the crab angle. To hold a course, turn until the ground-track marker sits under the bearing pointer. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12, 65')}</a></p>
