 <h2 id="navaids">Radio navigation aids</h2>
 <p>Ground navigation aids (NAVAIDs) are owned by the FAA, the military services and others; the FAA sets the standards for the aids used for instrument flight in US controlled airspace. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-24')}</a> <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-1')}</a> Disregard any indication from a transmitter that a <Term id="NOTAM">NOTAM</Term> or other notice says is unusable or out of service, however valid it looks. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-1')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Radio navigation aids compared" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>What each aid gives you <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-26–2-30')}</a> <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-2–1-1-8')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Aid</th><th scope="col">Gives</th><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">NDB (nondirectional beacon)</th><td>Bearing to the station, on the aircraft's automatic direction finder (ADF)</td><td>LF/MF, normally 190–535 kHz; UHF NDBs 275–287 MHz</td><td>Homing in a crosswind flies a curved path. Static, thunderstorms and night interference cause erratic bearings, and the ADF has no warning flag: monitor the identifier.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">VOR (VHF omnidirectional range)</th><td>360 radials: magnetic bearings from the station</td><td>VHF, 108.0–117.95 MHz</td><td>Line of sight, so range grows with altitude. Course alignment generally within ±1°. Identify by its Morse code or recorded voice identifier.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">TACAN (tactical air navigation)</th><td>Bearing and continuous DME slant range</td><td>UHF, 126 two-way channels, 962–1213 MHz</td><td>Developed by the military because VOR/DME suited neither unusual sites nor the pitch and roll of ships. Compact and transportable; Morse identifier every 35 seconds. Bearing to unlimited aircraft, distance to 120 at once.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">DME (distance measuring equipment)</th><td>Slant range to the station</td><td>UHF, 962–1213 MHz</td><td>Reliable to 199 NM at line-of-sight altitude, accurate to better than ½ NM or 3% of the distance, whichever is greater.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">VORTAC</th><td>VOR azimuth, TACAN azimuth and TACAN distance</td><td>VHF and UHF, paired</td><td>A TACAN-equipped aircraft gets distance from it automatically; the VOR and TACAN channels are paired so azimuth and distance come from the same station.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>A VOR <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> is identified by its magnetic bearing from the station: whatever its heading, an aircraft on the 090 <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> is due east of the station, and flying to the station on that <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> its magnetic course is 270. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-28')}</a> <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> gives the same picture from different technical principles, needs <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> airborne equipment and does not work through a VOR receiver. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-5')}</a> Line-of-sight aids are spaced so that the airway system has coverage at the minimum en route altitude. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-30')}</a> Each aid has a standard service volume, the airspace in which the FAA protects its signal; a conical area directly above the aid is generally not usable for navigation. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-8')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="fixes">Fixes</h2>
 <p>A fix is an accurate position. Without a NAVAID or radar, a fix takes two or more lines of position, such as two railroads crossing, each found on the chart. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-10')}</a> <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> gives a fix on its own: the <Term id="Radial">radial</Term> and the distance from a station whose position is known. Airborne radar can fix a position by <Term id="Relative bearing">relative bearing</Term> and range from a known landmark. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-14')}</a> With DME it is now routine to fly from one radial/DME fix to another, and it allows tighter separation and holding patterns. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-30')}</a> Named fixes are also defined by a VOR <Term id="Radial">radial</Term>, <Term id="TACAN">TACAN</Term> course or NDB bearing crossing another course. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-3')}</a> On a route, waypoints are fixes defined by latitude and longitude. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-1')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="gps">Satellite navigation</h2>
 <p>The Global Positioning System (GPS) is a space-based navigation system of 24 to 32 satellites in medium earth orbit that broadcast their exact positions. The receiver turns the signals into distances, and with at least three satellites computes its position; exact timing is essential, because a small time offset can move the solution miles. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-33')}</a> The Department of Defense operates the constellation and monitors the satellites; the Navy text names the US Air Force. The FAA's figures: the constellation is designed so at least five satellites are always visible, and four are needed for a three-dimensional position. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-17')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Integrity.</strong> <Term id="RAIM">Receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</Term> (<Term id="RAIM">RAIM</Term>) checks the solution using a satellite beyond those needed: at least five, or four with barometric aiding. Without <Term id="RAIM">RAIM</Term> the pilot has no assurance that the position is right. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-17')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Augmentation.</strong> The Wide Area Augmentation System (WAAS) augments GPS to give precision approaches, with a tolerance the Navy text gives as 25 ft laterally and vertically. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-33')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Outages and interference.</strong> Satellites can fail or drift, and outages are announced by <Term id="NOTAM">NOTAM</Term>. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('2-33')}</a> GPS signals are weak and vulnerable to unintentional interference and to deliberate jamming and spoofing, which can take away GPS, hybrid GPS/inertial navigation and RNP capability, corrupt displayed position, wind and ground speed, and affect conventional navigation where it relies on GPS. The US government also runs announced GPS test events. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-4')}</a></li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="ins">Inertial navigation</h2>
 <p>An inertial reference unit uses gyros and accelerometers to give attitude, position and velocity. Once aligned with a known position it computes position continuously, but its accuracy decays with time: this is drift. An inertial navigation system (INS) adds a navigation computer and flies a programmed series of waypoints. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-1-15')}</a> Inertial systems are often coupled with GPS or DME/DME to improve overall performance. <a href="#source-aim">{a('1-2-1')}</a> The T-45C's Global Positioning/Inertial Navigation Assembly (GINA) is such a system: it feeds the HSI's waypoint, offset, ground speed and wind information. <a href="#source-p1204">{fti('12, 71')}</a> An inertial system is self-contained, so it keeps navigating without GPS, but its error grows from the last good update; with GPS it is corrected continuously.</p>

 <h2 id="terms">Terms</h2>
 <GlossaryList items={[
  'Radial', 'Relative bearing', 'Track', 'Drift angle', 'Variation', 'Deviation', 'Slant range', 'Lead point',
