 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>The bearing pointer's head reads 045 and its tail 225. Which radial are you on, and which way is the station?</summary><p>The 225 radial, south-west of the station; the station is on a bearing of 045.</p></details>
 <details><summary>True course 270°, variation 10° west, no wind. What magnetic heading?</summary><p>280°: west is best, so add the variation.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are 8 NM from a TACAN at 20,000 ft and time a 1-minute DME change. Is the ground speed good?</summary><p>No. DME must be at least the altitude in thousands of feet, here 20 NM, or slant range distorts the check.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Leaving a 20 DME arc for a radial at 300 kt: how many radials of lead?</summary><p>60 ÷ 20 × 3 = 9 radials.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Joining a 12 DME arc outbound at 250 kt: when do you turn?</summary><p>At 9.5 DME: 12 minus 1% of 250.</p></details>
 <details><summary>On the inbound course, the bar starts drifting left. What do you do?</summary><p>A crosswind from the left: turn left about 20°, and when the bar centres take out half the correction.</p></details>
 <details><summary>On a T-45C with TACAN steering, the bar sits one dot right at 30 NM from the station. How far off course are you?</summary><p>About 2½ NM: one dot is 5°, and at 30 NM the radials are about ½ NM apart. The course is to your right.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Which MGRS reference is more precise, 16S GL 0182 or 16S GL 012823?</summary><p>The six-digit one, to 100 m; four digits name a 1,000 m square.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Your GPS position and a TACAN fix disagree by several miles. What do you do?</summary><p>Suspect the GPS (interference or spoofing is possible), navigate on the conventional aid, and tell ATC about the apparent GPS problem.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why fly a fly-over waypoint differently from a fly-by one?</summary><p>You must cross a fly-over waypoint before turning, so you overshoot and re-intercept the next leg; at a fly-by waypoint the turn starts early and cuts inside.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-navedtra"><strong>[1] US Navy, Air Traffic Controller, NAVEDTRA 14342A, chapter 2, Air Navigation and Aids to Air Navigation.</strong> Read on the Integrated Publishing mirror (navyaviation.tpub.com), 25 October 2026: <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Table-2-2-Geographic-Coordinates-81.html">geographic coordinates (p. 2-4)</a>; <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Units-Of-Measurement-84.html">units of measurement (p. 2-7)</a>; <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Vhf-Uhf-Omnidirectional-Ranges-104.html">NDB limits and VOR (p. 2-27)</a>; <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Tactical-Air-Navigation-Tacan-System-105.html">VOR and TACAN (p. 2-28)</a>; <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Distance-Measuring-Equipment-106.html">VORTAC and DME (p. 2-29)</a>; <a href="https://navyaviation.tpub.com/14342A/Global-Positioning-System-110.html">GPS (p. 2-33)</a>. Also cited from the same chapter: pp. 2-1 to 2-3, 2-5, 2-6, 2-10 to 2-14, 2-24, 2-26 and 2-30. A Navy controller-training course, cited for navigation fundamentals and NAVAIDs.</p>
 <p id="source-p1204"><strong>[2] Naval Air Training Command, CNATRA P-1204, Flight Training Instruction, Instrument Flight, T-45C (TS, ADV and IUT), (4-01) Original.</strong> Read on the Integrated Publishing mirror, navyflightmanuals.tpub.com/P-1204/, 25 October 2026: pp. 1, 12–13 (HSI), 64–80 (proceeding direct, intercepts, ground speed checks, arcs, point-to-point, waypoint offsets and GPS data). The edition read is an older revision of the T-45C instrument FTI; current CNATRA revisions may differ.</p>
 <p id="source-fm"><strong>[3] Headquarters, Department of the Army, FM 3-25.26, Map Reading and Land Navigation, 20 July 2001.</strong> Approved for public release. Read from an archive.org copy, 25 October 2026: p. 3-3 (datum note), 4-12 to 4-13 and 4-21 to 4-23 (MGRS), J-2 (GPS coordinate formats). Cited for the military grid, which all US services share.</p>
 <h3>Supplementary: FAA sources</h3>
 <p id="source-aim"><strong>[4] Federal Aviation Administration, Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM), current edition as published online.</strong> Inspected 25 October 2026 on faa.gov: <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap1_section_2.html">chapter 1, section 2, Performance-Based Navigation and Area Navigation</a> (paras 1-2-1, 1-2-3, 1-2-4); <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap1_section_1.html">chapter 1, section 1, Air Navigation Aids</a> (paras 1-1-1 to 1-1-8, 1-1-15, 1-1-17).</p>
 <p id="source-phak"><strong>[5] Federal Aviation Administration, Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, FAA-H-8083-25C, chapter 16, Navigation.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/18_phak_ch16.pdf">Read the chapter (faa.gov)</a>. Inspected 25 October 2026: pp. 16-5 to 16-15.</p>
 <p id="source-ifh"><strong>[6] Federal Aviation Administration, Instrument Flying Handbook, FAA-H-8083-15B.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/FAA-H-8083-15B.pdf">Read the handbook (faa.gov)</a>. Inspected 26 October 2026: p. 5-23 (the HSI as a view from above, TO/FROM), p. 9-13 (VOR CDI dot scale) and p. 9-14 (tracking, wind bracketing, station passage, course interception).</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/air-navigation-evidence.md">air navigation evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1–7 are original drawings and constructed examples, not reproductions of any publication's figures. Routes, positions and winds in constructed examples are invented.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for US military pilots. The Navy sources [1, 2] are training publications, one a controller course and one a training-aircraft instruction from an earlier revision; your NATOPS or service flight manual, unit procedures and current flight information publications govern. The Army manual [3] is cited only for the military grid. The FAA AIM, PHAK and Instrument Flying Handbook [4–6] are the US civil references that military aircraft also follow in the National Airspace System. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
